WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.240
In 1959, a laboratory in Paris counted the chromosomes in cells from nine children

2
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.240
with Down syndrome.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.320
It found 47, not the usual 46.

4
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.780
One chromosome too many.

5
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.920
How does a cell end up with an extra one?

6
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.560
The answer begins in meiosis, when a pair of chromosomes fails to part.

7
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:31.083
A cell makes sperm or eggs in two divisions.

8
00:00:31.923 --> 00:00:36.003
At meiosis I, the two homologues of each pair separate.

9
00:00:36.743 --> 00:00:41.263
At meiosis II, the two sister chromatids of each chromosome separate.

10
00:00:42.443 --> 00:00:46.823
Nondisjunction is a failure of chromosomes to part when a cell divides.

11
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:50.023
Where it happens changes the result.

12
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:58.447
Follow chromosome 21 in one human spermatocyte, and call the father's homologues 21A

13
00:00:58.447 --> 00:01:05.787
and 21B. Suppose they fail to separate at meiosis I, so both go to the same cell.

14
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:08.527
Meiosis II runs normally.

15
00:01:09.187 --> 00:01:14.147
That cell gives two sperm that are disomic, meaning each has two copies.

16
00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:18.407
The other cell has none, so its two sperm are nullisomic.

17
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:24.347
Now fail at meiosis II instead, with meiosis I normal.

18
00:01:25.247 --> 00:01:32.667
In one cell the sisters of 21A fail to part, giving one disomic sperm and one with

19
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:37.527
none. The other cell divides normally and gives two normal sperm.

20
00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:44.867
So a meiosis I error gives two disomic sperm, and a meiosis II error gives one.

21
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:51.067
Joined to a normal egg, a disomic sperm gives a zygote with three copies.

22
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.370
The two errors leave different fingerprints near the centromere, because homologues

23
00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.410
differ there and sisters do not.

24
00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.710
Take a DNA marker tightly linked to the centromere of chromosome 21.

25
00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:10.430
The father carries alleles 1 and 2, and the mother carries 3 and 4.

26
00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:17.670
Child A types 1, 3, 4: one paternal allele, but two different maternal alleles.

27
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.430
The extra chromosome is maternal and carries both of her homologues, so the error

28
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.290
was at maternal meiosis I.

29
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:33.050
Child B types 1, 3, 3: two copies of one maternal allele.

30
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.890
The sisters stayed together, which is consistent with maternal meiosis II.

31
00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:45.310
One caveat. A crossover between the marker and the centromere can make sisters

32
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.370
differ, so one error can look like the other.

33
00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:55.630
Keep the rule: two homologues together point to meiosis I, and two sisters together

34
00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.310
point to meiosis II.
